{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>QA said the modal was keyboard accessible. Tabbing past the last field said otherwise</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team ships a "Delete project" confirmation modal. Tab moves through its fields correctly, name input, checkbox, Cancel, Delete, so the ticket gets marked keyboard-accessible and closed. A week later someone reports that pressing Tab one more time, right after landing on the Delete button, doesn't loop back to the name input. It moves focus onto a link in the page behind the modal, a page that's still fully there, still fully interactive, just visually covered by the dialog on top of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The modal was never actually "trapping" anything. It handled Tab moving forward through its own fields and stopped there, the one moment that mattered most, the boundary where focus tries to leave, was never handled at all.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      "The fields tab through in order" and "focus is trapped inside the dialog" are not the same claim, and a lot of modal implementations only actually build the first one. The second one requires explicitly intercepting Tab at both ends of the tab sequence, wrapping last back to first and first back to last, plus a separate, easy-to-forget obligation: returning focus to whatever triggered the dialog once it closes.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The deeper failure mode is building a trap that only reacts to keyboard-driven Tab and nothing else, or conversely, one that reacts to <em>any</em> focus change by yanking focus back on a timer. Both are wrong in different directions: the first leaves a real leak for sighted keyboard users, the second can fight a screen reader's own virtual-cursor navigation, which moves an internal reading position independently of DOM focus and doesn't expect the page to fight it.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Two separate obligations, both required: wrap the ends, return focus on close</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A correct focus trap does two things. First, Tab from the last focusable element inside the dialog moves focus to the first one instead of leaving the dialog, and Shift+Tab from the first element moves to the last one, so focus can never escape while the dialog is open. Second, when the dialog closes, focus goes back to whatever element opened it, the button the user's hand is already near, not to the top of the page.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Implementing the wrap correctly means capturing the <code>keydown</code> event for Tab, checking whether the currently focused element is the last (or first) focusable element inside the dialog's container, and if so, calling <code>preventDefault()</code> and explicitly moving focus with <code>.focus()</code>. Implementing the return means saving a reference to <code>document.activeElement</code> at the moment the dialog opens, that's the trigger, and calling <code>.focus()</code> on it explicitly when the dialog closes. Neither of these happens automatically; a dialog that's simply hidden or removed from the DOM leaves focus wherever the browser's default fallback puts it, usually <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      A common way the wrap breaks in practice: computing the list of focusable elements once, at dialog-open time, and never recomputing it. If a disabled submit button becomes enabled mid-interaction, or a conditional field appears, the cached first/last elements go stale and the trap starts wrapping to the wrong place, or to an element that's no longer actually focusable. The robust version recomputes the focusable-elements list at the moment Tab is pressed, not once up front.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The trap that fought the screen reader instead of the page</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      An earlier version of the same modal did attempt to handle the escape case, but the wrong way: instead of intercepting the Tab key, it listened for any <code>focus</code> event anywhere in the document and, if the newly focused element was outside the dialog, immediately called <code>.focus()</code> back onto the dialog's first field. For a sighted mouse user this looked fine. For a screen reader user navigating in browse mode, where the assistive technology moves its own internal reading cursor across the page independently of where DOM focus actually is, this handler fired constantly, yanking real focus back into the dialog every time their virtual cursor happened to pass an element outside it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The reporter described it as the page "fighting" their screen reader, every attempt to read past the dialog's boundary got forcibly interrupted. The team's first instinct was to fix the timing; the actual fix was architectural, stop reacting to focus events after the fact and instead intercept the Tab keydown before the browser moves focus at all, which only fires on an actual keyboard Tab press and never on the screen reader's own internal navigation.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The retrospective's real lesson was that "trap focus" had been treated as one job when it's really two, keep keyboard <code>Tab</code> cycling inside the dialog, and separately, mark the dialog's boundary declaratively (<code>role="dialog"</code>, <code>aria-modal="true"</code>, and <code>inert</code> or an equivalent on everything outside it) so assistive technology understands the boundary on its own terms instead of the page trying to enforce it by force-focusing elements after the fact.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

