{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>The native &lt;dialog&gt; element, and why hand-rolling still happens anyway</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The native <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> element's <code>.showModal()</code> method gets you a correct focus trap and closing on Escape for free, in modern browsers, with no hand-written keydown handler at all. If a new modal doesn't need custom open/close animation choreography that <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> can't easily do, start there instead of building the trap by hand.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Teams still hand-roll modals for real reasons: custom enter/exit animations, needing the dialog to coexist with other overlay layers in a specific stacking order, or supporting older browsers. When hand-rolling is the actual constraint, the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide's dialog pattern is the spec to build against, not intuition, it defines the exact Tab/Shift+Tab wrapping behavior and the focus-return-to-trigger requirement this case demonstrates.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      A hand-rolled trap also needs to hide the rest of the page from assistive technology, not just visually cover it, or a screen reader's virtual cursor can still browse "through" a backdrop into content that's supposed to be inert while the dialog is open. Applying <code>inert</code> to everything outside the dialog (or <code>aria-hidden="true"</code> on sibling containers, for browsers without <code>inert</code> support) closes that gap; a semi-transparent visual overlay by itself does nothing for the accessibility tree.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

