{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">A focus trap needs two things: Tab from the last field wraps to the first (and back), and closing the dialog returns focus to whatever opened it. Fields tabbing through in order alone isn't a trap.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Intercept Tab at the keydown level and check against a freshly computed focusable-elements list, not a cached one from dialog-open time — and explicitly save and restore focus to the trigger element on close, the browser won't do either for you.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Reacting to focus events after the fact to yank focus back can fight a screen reader's own virtual-cursor navigation. Mark the boundary declaratively with role="dialog", aria-modal="true", and inert on everything outside it, so assistive tech understands the boundary instead of the page enforcing it by force.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
